ere is  no injuries it's an  additional misdemeanor crime for the  fault of the accident and for leaving  if you leave and there are injuries it  rises to a felony  and that's a b level felony which is i  believe two to 20 years  if you are convicted as such so the  important piece on this  is you know you could be involved in a  misdemeanor accident but  elevate it to a felony because you panic  for  various reasons you may not have  insurance you may have warrants you may  be intoxicated  i guess there's a plethora of reasons  associated with that but there's no  excuse for those actions  especially if somebody's injured and  we've just seen so many of those uh  collisions recently uh let's also not  talk about you know what john  yeah sure let me just build off that  back to your original question  so we have a robust investigation  all those um i think it's important for  people to realize that  you know you do a hit and run you think  you're you're going to easily get away  with that and  what is most common is we get  information off your plate or  registration or video cameras  and the the success that we experience  here at lvmpd and locating the drivers  of the other vehicle is very high  so it's important for people to realize  that let's finally talk about the budget  sheriff  last year during the depths of the  pandemic metro had to make a lot of  budget cuts a lot of tough decisions  hiring freezes where do things stand now  as the valley starts to come out of the  pandemic  yeah you're right we did have to make  some significant cuts and  we had some hiring freezes both on the  policing side the corrections side and  the civilian ranks and we even  eliminated some programs  we anticipated the sales tax to be  dire which is a significant number  associated with our budget but  in with the federal stimulus it wasn't  as bad as we predicted  so with that knowledge and ability to  move forward  and to go back to our six academy a year  program we are increasing hiring  and trying to get back to where we were  previous to the covid pandemic  but that doesn't happen overnight it's  like turning a cruise ship per se  we uh it's going to take us around 10 to  12 months to get back to our original  numbers  that we experienced before um and  on that note we've got a few seconds  left here we're in the middle of  national police week where we recognize  the work of  the men and women in law enforcement is  it getting harder to  to retain and also just hire folks to  join the force right now  yeah it is um you know we had a we had  not a significant additional number of  people separate from the police  department during the pandemic or last  year on  sue's calendar year last year we  averaged right around 170 on the  policing side  on an annual basis we had additional 60  people uh  leave last year so and we are having  trouble recruiting now that could be a  combination of  uh people's perception of the police  george floyd post events and then you  know people were in fear of the cove  we were essential workers we had to go  to work on a  daily basis just like normal business  where other people  had the ability to remain at home and  sequester themselves from the pandemic  and we didn't have that luxury so there  was some fear associated with that  um now we're moving forward people are  getting inoculated and  and hopefully we can increase those  numbers as we  continue to go forward now we're still  dealing with the perception of the  police  as a as a whole and but here locally  i think our communities wholeheartedly  and hopefully that will be a problem  sheriff we thank you once again for  joining us this morning  if you at home have a question for  sheriff lombardo you can submit it on  eight newsnow dot com find out if a  question is answered every wednesday  sheriff thank you once again for joining  us this morning  6 40

